{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06113","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06113","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06113","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuwen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liaoman","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Liaoman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanming","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changmiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Changmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiwei","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Feiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and Discriminative Cancer Survival Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:59:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T05:29:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:36:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Survival analysis plays a vital role in making clinical decisions. However, the models currently in use are often difficult to interpret, which reduces their usefulness in clinical settings. Prototype learning presents a potential solution, yet traditional methods focus on local similarities and static matching, neglecting the broader tumor context and lacking strong semantic alignment with genomic data. To overcome these issues, we introduce an innovative prototype-based multimodal framework, FeatProto, aimed at enhancing cancer survival prediction by addressing significant limitations in current prototype learning methodologies within pathology. Our framework establishes a unified feature prototype space that integrates both global and local features of whole slide images (WSI) with genomic profiles. This integration facilitates traceable and interpretable decision-making processes. Our approach includes three main innovations: (1) A robust phenotype representation that merges critical patches with global context, harmonized with genomic data to minimize local bias. (2) An Exponential Prototype Update Strategy (EMA ProtoUp) that sustains stable cross-modal associations and employs a wandering mechanism to adapt prototypes flexibly to tumor heterogeneity. (3) A hierarchical prototype matching scheme designed to capture global centrality, local typicality, and cohort-level trends, thereby refining prototype inference. Comprehensive evaluations on four publicly available cancer datasets indicate that our method surpasses current leading unimodal and multimodal survival prediction techniques in both accuracy and interpretability, providing a new perspective on prototype learning for critical medical applications. Our source code is available at https://github.com/JSLiam94/FeatProto."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06113","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:01:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Ghugare","name":"Ghugare, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger Creus","familyName":"Castanyer","name":"Castanyer, Roger Creus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Catherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathryn","familyName":"Wantlin","name":"Wantlin, Kathryn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Schofield","name":"Schofield, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Narasimhan","name":"Narasimhan, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Eysenbach","name":"Eysenbach, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BuilderBench: The Building Blocks of Intelligent Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T02:52:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T13:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's AI models learn primarily through mimicry and refining, so it is not surprising that they struggle to solve problems beyond the limits set by existing data. To solve novel problems, agents should acquire skills by exploring and learning through experience. Finding a scalable learning mechanism for developing agents that learn through interaction remains a major open problem. In this work, we introduce BuilderBench, a benchmark to accelerate research into agent training that centers open-ended exploration. BuilderBench requires agents to learn how to build any structure using blocks. BuilderBench is equipped with (1) a simulator of a robot interacting with various physical blocks, and (2) a task-suite with over 50 diverse target structures that are carefully curated to test an understanding of physics, mathematics, and long-horizon planning. Agents are provided with a target structure at the start, and can interact with the environment for multiple episodes to experiment and learn various skills for building the structure. Solving these tasks requires \\emph{embodied reasoning} in a way that is not reflected in words but rather in actions, experimenting with different strategies and piecing them together. Our experiments with multiple state-of-the-art frontier language model based agents and tabula rasa reinforcement learning algorithms show that these agents cannot solve any of the non-trivial tasks in the BuilderBench. Our analysis throws light on the lack of exploration abilities in these models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Blogpost: https://rajghugare19.github.io/builderbench and Code: https://github.com/rajghugare19/builderbench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manolis","familyName":"Mylonas","name":"Mylonas, Manolis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charalampia","familyName":"Zerva","name":"Zerva, Charalampia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evlampios","familyName":"Apostolidis","name":"Apostolidis, Evlampios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasileios","familyName":"Mezaris","name":"Mezaris, Vasileios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SD-MVSum: Script-Driven Multimodal Video Summarization Method and Datasets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:03:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:31:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-07T12:24:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-08T00:54:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we present a method and two large-scale datasets for Script-Driven Multimodal Video Summarization. The proposed method, SD-MVSum, builds on our earlier SD-VSum method for script-driven video summarization, which considered just the visual content of the video. SD-MVSum takes into account, in addition to the visual modality, the relevance of the user-provided script with the spoken content (i.e., audio transcript) of the video. The dependence between each considered pair of data modalities, i.e., script-video and script-transcript, is modeled using a new weighted cross-modal attention mechanism. This mechanism explicitly exploits the semantic similarity between the paired modalities in order to promote the parts of the full-length video with the highest relevance to the user-provided script. Furthermore, we extend two large-scale datasets for script-driven (S-VideoXum) and generic (MrHiSum) video summarization, to make them suitable for training and evaluation of script-driven multimodal video summarization methods. Experimental comparisons document the competitiveness of the proposed SD-MVSum method against other SotA approaches for script-driven and generic video summarization. Our new method and extended datasets are available at: https://github.com/IDT-ITI/SD-MVSum."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-08T12:51:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06139","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06139","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06139","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zanyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dengyang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Dengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liuzhuozheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Liuzhuozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sizhe","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Sizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengzu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guang","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Guang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingdong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deforming Videos to Masks: Flow Matching for Referring Video Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:14:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T09:15:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:34:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Referring Video Object Segmentation (RVOS) requires segmenting specific objects in a video guided by a natural language description. The core challenge of RVOS is to anchor abstract linguistic concepts onto a specific set of pixels and continuously segment them through the complex dynamics of a video. Faced with this difficulty, prior work has often decomposed the task into a pragmatic `locate-then-segment' pipeline. However, this cascaded design creates an information bottleneck by simplifying semantics into coarse geometric prompts (e.g, point), and struggles to maintain temporal consistency as the segmenting process is often decoupled from the initial language grounding. To overcome these fundamental limitations, we propose FlowRVS, a novel framework that reconceptualizes RVOS as a conditional continuous flow problem. This allows us to harness the inherent strengths of pretrained T2V models, fine-grained pixel control, text-video semantic alignment, and temporal coherence. Instead of conventional generating from noise to mask or directly predicting mask, we reformulate the task by learning a direct, language-guided deformation from a video's holistic representation to its target mask. Our one-stage, generative approach achieves new state-of-the-art results across all major RVOS benchmarks. Specifically, achieving a J\u0026amp;F of 51.1 in MeViS (+1.6 over prior SOTA) and 73.3 in the zero shot Ref-DAVIS17 (+2.7), demonstrating the significant potential of modeling video understanding tasks as continuous deformation processes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06139","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongryool","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jongryool","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byungil","familyName":"Koh","name":"Koh, Byungil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoshik","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hoshik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"cMPI: Using CXL Memory Sharing for MPI One-Sided and Two-Sided Inter-Node Communications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-13T06:18:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:22:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3712285.3759816","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Message Passing Interface (MPI) is a foundational programming model for high-performance computing. MPI libraries traditionally employ network interconnects (e.g., Ethernet and InfiniBand) and network protocols (e.g., TCP and RoCE) with complex software stacks for cross-node communication. We present cMPI, the first work to optimize MPI point-to-point communication (both one-sided and two-sided) using CXL memory sharing on a real CXL platform, transforming cross-node communication into memory transactions and data copies within CXL memory, bypassing traditional network protocols. We analyze performance across various interconnects and find that CXL memory sharing achieves 7.2x-8.1x lower latency than TCP-based interconnects deployed in small- and medium-scale clusters. We address challenges of CXL memory sharing for MPI communication, including data object management over the dax representation [50], cache coherence, and atomic operations. Overall, cMPI outperforms TCP over standard Ethernet NIC and high-end SmartNIC by up to 49x and 72x in latency and bandwidth, respectively, for small messages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:46Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2511.11581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2511.11581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2511.11581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burkhard","familyName":"Ringlein","name":"Ringlein, Burkhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"van Lunteren","name":"van Lunteren, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radu","familyName":"Stoica","name":"Stoica, Radu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Parnell","name":"Parnell, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Anatomy of a Triton Attention Kernel"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; D.2; C.4; C.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:34:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:00:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A long-standing goal in both industry and academia is to develop an LLM inference platform that is portable across hardware architectures, eliminates the need for low-level hand-tuning, and still delivers best-in-class efficiency. In this work, we demonstrate that portable, efficient cross-platform LLM inference is indeed possible and share our experience. We develop a state-of-the-art paged attention kernel, the core performance-critical component of many LLM deployments, that builds exclusively on the domain-specific just-in-time compiled language Triton to achieve state-of-the-art performance on both NVIDIA and AMD GPUs. We describe our high-level approach, the key algorithmic and system-level improvements, the parameter auto-tuning required to unlock efficiency, and the integrations into a popular inference server that are necessary to bring the performance of a generic Triton attention kernel from 19.7% of the state-of-the-art to 105.9%. Our results highlight how open-source domain-specific languages can be leveraged to unlock model portability across different GPU vendors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-11-18T04:04:45Z","registered":"2025-11-18T04:04:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T04:04:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imranur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Imranur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rayhanur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Md Rayhanur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relative Positioning Based Code Chunking Method For Rich Context Retrieval In Repository Level Code Completion Task With Code Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:44:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code completion can help developers improve efficiency and ease the development lifecycle. Although code completion is available in modern integrated development environments (IDEs), research lacks in determining what makes a good context for code completion based on the information available to the IDEs for the large language models (LLMs) to perform better. In this paper, we describe an effective context collection strategy to assist the LLMs in performing better at code completion tasks. The key idea of our strategy is to preprocess the repository into smaller code chunks and later use syntactic and semantic similarity-based code chunk retrieval with relative positioning. We found that code chunking and relative positioning of the chunks in the final context improve the performance of code completion tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Context Collection Workshop co-located with ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:27Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:40:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmoy","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Tanmoy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxue","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xiaoxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan Chen Zhengyu","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Bryan Chen Zhengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaojun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiyao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koji","familyName":"Inoue","name":"Inoue, Koji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryuichi","familyName":"Sumida","name":"Sumida, Ryuichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuya","familyName":"Kawahara","name":"Kawahara, Tatsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimin","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Jimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyung","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Jaehyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadya Yuki","familyName":"Wangsajaya","name":"Wangsajaya, Nadya Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pham Minh","familyName":"Duc","name":"Duc, Pham Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ojasva","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Ojasva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Palash","familyName":"Nandi","name":"Nandi, Palash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyan","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Xiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wiwik","familyName":"Karlina","name":"Karlina, Wiwik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan","familyName":"Luong","name":"Luong, Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keertana Arun","familyName":"Vasan","name":"Vasan, Keertana Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy Ka-Wei","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Roy Ka-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nancy F.","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Nancy F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MMA-ASIA: A Multilingual and Multimodal Alignment Framework for Culturally-Grounded Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:12:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are now used worldwide, yet their multimodal understanding and reasoning often degrade outside Western, high-resource settings. We propose MMA-ASIA, a comprehensive framework to evaluate LLMs' cultural awareness with a focus on Asian contexts. MMA-ASIA centers on a human-curated, multilingual, and multimodally aligned multiple-choice benchmark covering 8 Asian countries and 10 languages, comprising 27,000 questions; over 79 percent require multi-step reasoning grounded in cultural context, moving beyond simple memorization. To our knowledge, this is the first dataset aligned at the input level across three modalities: text, image (visual question answering), and speech. This enables direct tests of cross-modal transfer. Building on this benchmark, we propose a five-dimensional evaluation protocol that measures: (i) cultural-awareness disparities across countries, (ii) cross-lingual consistency, (iii) cross-modal consistency, (iv) cultural knowledge generalization, and (v) grounding validity. To ensure rigorous assessment, a Cultural Awareness Grounding Validation Module detects \"shortcut learning\" by checking whether the requisite cultural knowledge supports correct answers. Finally, through comparative model analysis, attention tracing, and an innovative Vision-ablated Prefix Replay (VPR) method, we probe why models diverge across languages and modalities, offering actionable insights for building culturally reliable multimodal LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:24Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:40:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Hanlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linlin","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Linlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyuan","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Leyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taihao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Taihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Centering Emotion Hotspots: Multimodal Local-Global Fusion and Cross-Modal Alignment for Emotion Recognition in Conversations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:11:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Emotion Recognition in Conversations (ERC) is hard because discriminative evidence is sparse, localized, and often asynchronous across modalities. We center ERC on emotion hotspots and present a unified model that detects per-utterance hotspots in text, audio, and video, fuses them with global features via Hotspot-Gated Fusion, and aligns modalities using a routed Mixture-of-Aligners; a cross-modal graph encodes conversational structure. This design focuses modeling on salient spans, mitigates misalignment, and preserves context. Experiments on standard ERC benchmarks show consistent gains over strong baselines, with ablations confirming the contributions of HGF and MoA. Our results point to a hotspot-centric view that can inform future multimodal learning, offering a new perspective on modality fusion in ERC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review for ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:21Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:40:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nouar","familyName":"Aldahoul","name":"Aldahoul, Nouar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasir","familyName":"Zaki","name":"Zaki, Yasir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward a Safer Web: Multilingual Multi-Agent LLMs for Mitigating Adversarial Misinformation Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:09:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid spread of misinformation on digital platforms threatens public discourse, emotional stability, and decision-making. While prior work has explored various adversarial attacks in misinformation detection, the specific transformations examined in this paper have not been systematically studied. In particular, we investigate language-switching across English, French, Spanish, Arabic, Hindi, and Chinese, followed by translation. We also study query length inflation preceding summarization and structural reformatting into multiple-choice questions. In this paper, we present a multilingual, multi-agent large language model framework with retrieval-augmented generation that can be deployed as a web plugin into online platforms. Our work underscores the importance of AI-driven misinformation detection in safeguarding online factual integrity against diverse attacks, while showcasing the feasibility of plugin-based deployment for real-world web applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:19Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:40:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08601","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08601","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08601","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Kaliber AI"],"givenName":"Aneesh","familyName":"Jonelagadda","name":"Jonelagadda, Aneesh","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Kaliber AI"],"givenName":"Christina","familyName":"Hahn","name":"Hahn, Christina","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Kaliber AI"],"givenName":"Haoze","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haoze","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Kaliber AI"],"givenName":"Salvatore","familyName":"Penachio","name":"Penachio, Salvatore","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mnemosyne: An Unsupervised, Human-Inspired Long-Term Memory Architecture for Edge-Based LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:32:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-term memory is essential for natural, realistic dialogue. However, current large language model (LLM) memory systems rely on either brute-force context expansion or static retrieval pipelines that fail on edge-constrained devices. We introduce Mnemosyne, an unsupervised, human-inspired long-term memory architecture designed for edge-based LLMs. Our approach uses graph-structured storage, modular substance and redundancy filters, memory committing and pruning mechanisms, and probabilistic recall with temporal decay and refresh processes modeled after human memory. Mnemosyne also introduces a concentrated \"core summary\" efficiently derived from a fixed-length subset of the memory graph to capture the user's personality and other domain-specific long-term details such as, using healthcare application as an example, post-recovery ambitions and attitude towards care. Unlike existing retrieval-augmented methods, Mnemosyne is designed for use in longitudinal healthcare assistants, where repetitive and semantically similar but temporally distinct conversations are limited by naive retrieval. In experiments with longitudinal healthcare dialogues, Mnemosyne demonstrates the highest win rate of 65.8% in blind human evaluations of realism and long-term memory capability compared to a baseline RAG win rate of 31.1%. Mnemosyne also achieves current highest LoCoMo benchmark scores in temporal reasoning and single-hop retrieval compared to other same-backboned techniques. Further, the average overall score of 54.6% was second highest across all methods, beating commonly used Mem0 and OpenAI baselines among others. This demonstrates that improved factual recall, enhanced temporal reasoning, and much more natural user-facing responses can be feasible with an edge-compatible and easily transferable unsupervised memory architecture."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08601","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:14Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:40:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06280","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06280","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06280","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zohaib Hasan","familyName":"Siddiqui","name":"Siddiqui, Zohaib Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayam","familyName":"Nadeem","name":"Nadeem, Dayam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Masudur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mohammad Masudur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Nadeem","name":"Nadeem, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahab Saquib","familyName":"Sohail","name":"Sohail, Shahab Saquib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beenish Moalla","familyName":"Chaudhry","name":"Chaudhry, Beenish Moalla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:20:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision language models (VLMs), such as CLIP and OpenCLIP, can encode and reflect stereotypical associations between medical professions and demographic attributes learned from web-scale data. We present an evaluation protocol for healthcare settings that quantifies associated biases and assesses their operational risk. Our methodology (i) defines a taxonomy spanning clinicians and allied healthcare roles (e.g., surgeon, cardiologist, dentist, nurse, pharmacist, technician), (ii) curates a profession-aware prompt suite to probe model behavior, and (iii) benchmarks demographic skew against a balanced face corpus. Empirically, we observe consistent demographic biases across multiple roles and vision models. Our work highlights the importance of bias identification in critical domains such as healthcare as AI-enabled hiring and workforce analytics can have downstream implications for equity, compliance, and patient trust."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06280","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:36Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":12,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=doi%3A%28%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05476%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05652%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.06113%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.06139%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.06280%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.06288%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.08601%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.08605%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.08606%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.08608%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2510.08610%22+OR+%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2511.11581%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100"}}